From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0132-a-refused-buffer-waits-once-and-collectors-help.patch

Source: 0132-a-refused-buffer-waits-once-and-collectors-help.patch
Moved by hand: the release hook is set beside upstream's new SetPendingCpuWrites in the rasterizer's constructor

caches: a refused buffer waits for the GPU once, and the collectors help at the end of the frame

0131 keeps the session alive. This tries to give the buffer what it asked for first.

The images the collector deleted and the buffers replaced by a join keep their memory for a while:
images until TickFrame sees their tick, buffers for 8 frames. A growing buffer's old copy is one of
those, about the size being refused. ReleaseFinished destroys them, right after a wait for the GPU.

While a draw or dispatch looks up its buffers (may_wait_for_memory), a refused buffer waits once in
the 250 ms pause: Scheduler::Finish, ReleaseFinished in both caches, and the retry. Staging goes in
that retry too: MemoryAllocator::ReleaseRefused lets go of what is finished on its way. Both cache
locks are held there (PrepareDraw, DispatchCompute) and on the same thread as the scheduler. Nothing
recorded is lost: images were filled before, descriptors come after, and the check 0095 added binds
the geometry again when the tick moved.

Everywhere else, the refusal only leaves a note. ObtainCPUBuffer clears the flag around its lookup,
so a query cannot wait even if it ran inside a draw's setup. A submission from inside
Scheduler::EndRenderPass would end the pass twice, and a query sync can hold the buffer cache's lock
with no claim on the texture cache's. The source does not reach SyncValues from EndRenderPass, only
from NotifyWFI (WaitForIdle and fences); the entries of run 53 are a scan of the stack and can be
stale. This does not depend on which.

The note is read at the end of RasterizerVulkan::TickFrame, where no cache lock is held, and both are
taken together, as a draw does. There the texture cache deletes up to 32 images unused for 3 frames
with nothing to copy back, stopping when one left a hole of the size wanted. The normal collector is
not used: it may ask for staging for dirty images, and its marks already keep it at 10 frames (run 53:
28 images, 69 MiB past that). The buffer collector runs in aggressive mode, then the wait and
ReleaseFinished. The bindings 0131 cut are set dirty, so the vertex and index buffers ask again.
Storage and texture bindings do at every draw, uniforms when the guest binds them.

The collectors never run during a draw: it holds image views and bindings.

Host conditional rendering ends before the destruction. The predicate's VkBuffer can be among those
destroyed, and resuming would use it. The draws go unconditionally until the guest sets a condition.

Cost: a GPU wait at most every 250 ms while memory stays short, and textures of 3 frames ago to load
again. EDEN_NX2_BUFFER_WAIT=0 turns all of this off and leaves 0131.

Needs 0131. Proven with stubbed headers as 0131. Not built, not run on a console. Likely: the wait
frees a hole for a buffer that grew. A guess: how often. With 1 MiB left in the pool and the largest
hole at 6 MiB, the join may still be refused, and 0131 is what is left.

Sources: the wait and rebind of 0095, the try-lock rule of the refusal hook in
tools/prepare-vulkan-port.py, gpu_sentenced_images in TextureCache::TickFrame, the eviction steps of
TextureCache::RunGarbageCollector; run 53 of 2026-11-05.

